age></element-citation></ref><ref id="B12"><label>12</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Park</surname><given-names>MI</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Clinical usefulness of high-resolution manometry</article-title><source>Korean J Neurogastroenterol Motil</source><year>2009</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>107</fpage><lpage>115</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="B13"><label>13</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ertekin</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Aydogdu</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Yuceyar</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Kiylioglu</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Tarlaci</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Uludag</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Pathophysiological mechanisms of oropharyngeal dysphagia in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</article-title><source>Brain</source><year>2000</year><volume>123</volume><fpage>125</fpage><lpage>140</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10611127</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B14"><label>14</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kuhnlein</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Gdynia</surname><given-names>HJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Sperfeld</surname><given-names>AD</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Diagnosis and treatment of bulbar symptoms in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis</article-title><source>Nat Clin Pract Neurol</source><year>2008</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>366</fpage><lpage>374</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18560390</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Video-fluoroscopic swallow study shows residues in valleculae and piriform sinus (white arrow) and tracheal aspiration (black arrow).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jnm-16-319-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>High resolutional esophageal manometric findings. Upper esophageal sphincter (UES) does not show relaxation on swallowing (white arrow). After trying several times, the UES becomes relaxed (black arrow). No other abnormal esophageal motility is found.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="jnm-16-319-g002"/></fig></floats-group></article>